Add a custom-attribute API and a mount-only entry point

Two gaps, both of which stop a project building anything on top of this port.

1. Custom attributes are unreachable.

LittleFS user attributes are the idiomatic place for POSIX-style metadata --
SPEC.md describes them as intended for "timestamps, hashes", they ride the
entry's metadata so rename moves them and delete drops them, and this port
already uses one ('t') for mtime. But nothing outside the port can reach them:
`_efs[]` is file-scope static, esp_littlefs_by_label() is static, and
littlefs_api.h is under PRIV_INCLUDE_DIRS. ESP-IDF's VFS cannot route around it
either, having no chmod or attribute member in esp_vfs_fs_ops_t -- IDF's own
vfs/test_apps/main/test_vfs_access.c carries a TODO saying as much.

esp_littlefs_setattr/getattr/removeattr are modelled directly on the port's
existing esp_littlefs_update_mtime_attr()/esp_littlefs_get_mtime_attr(), taking
the same sem_take(efs) lock and the same label lookup. Paths are
mount-relative, matching the rest of the internal API.

2. Mounting and registering cannot be separated.

esp_vfs_littlefs_register() mounts the filesystem *and* registers the driver at
a base path. A VFS stacked on top of this one -- adding permission checks, an
overlay, a union mount -- needs the first without the second: it has to be the
only name in the namespace, because if the layer beneath also has a base path
then that path reaches the filesystem with the upper layer's checks bypassed.

esp_littlefs_mount() does the mount and hands back the ops table and context so
a caller can invoke the driver directly. It is esp_vfs_littlefs_register()
minus its final esp_vfs_register_fs(), and that function could reasonably be
reimplemented in terms of it.

Note the public header now includes esp_vfs_ops.h, to name esp_vfs_fs_ops_t.
`vfs` is currently in priv_requires; a maintainer may prefer it public, or
prefer the out-params typed as void** to avoid the include.

